# wrench_zigzag.cam — wrench_offset.cam, pinned to the other answer (Phase 28 slice 3;
# DECISIONS.md → "Phase 28 slice 3: the patterns…"). Same part, same tool, same
# stepover; one word different: `pattern=zigzag`.
#
# Parallel passes along the wrench's long side, clipped to where the tool's
# centre may be — a radius past the open silhouette, a radius inside the box
# opening's rim — alternating direction, and joined ALONG that boundary where
# one pass ends beside the next one's start instead of lifting. Nothing stands
# on this face, so there is no closing pass: that is only ever cut beside a
# wall, an island or a keep-out.
#
# `nacs --patterns wrench_offset.cam` prints this program's numbers beside the
# default's. The engine shows; this line is the author choosing.
units mm
feed 800
plunge 300
rapid_z 5
clearance_z 25
spindle 3000

# The bar: 262 x 60, 9 thick, a 1mm skim over the wrench's top at z=0.
stock Box(w=262, l=60, h=9, x=0, y=0, z=1)

tool T5 Endmill(dia=10.0)

run End(tool=T5, surface=Top, stepover=5.0, pattern=zigzag)
